<mujoco model="overboard_bench_rig">
  <!--
    Overboard bench rig plant model — wave-0, desk-scale.

    A sensored outrunner bolted face-on to a plate, the plate clamped to a desk
    edge, and a disc of known inertia on the shaft. One hinge, one actuator.
    That is the whole vehicle here, and it is deliberately the simplest plant
    that still exercises the real signal path.

    WHY THIS MODEL EXISTS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT FOR
    --------------------------------------------
    It is NOT a scale model of the board and it does not predict anything about
    riding. It exists for two jobs:

      1. Fit a motor model (kt, rotor inertia, friction) from measurements, so
         the identification *method* is proven on hardware we own before it is
         pointed at a hub motor we do not.

      2. Measure the imperfection profile of the Pi -> CAN -> VESC path:
         actuation delay, current-loop lag, wheel-rate quantum and update rate,
         IMU noise. Those live in sim/scenarios/imperfections.py, where every
         value in STAGE0_PLACEHOLDER is currently an ICD 12 placeholder rather
         than a fitted number. Replacing that profile is what closes SR-SIM-3,
         and none of it needs a board.

    WHAT TRANSFERS TO THE BOARD, AND WHAT DOES NOT
    ----------------------------------------------
    This distinction is the whole point of the rig, so it is stated here rather
    than left to be inferred:

      TRANSFERS      the imperfection profile. Same Pi, same CAN stack, same
                     Little FOCer, same RT kernel. Delay and quantisation are
                     properties of that path, not of the motor hanging off it.

      DOES NOT       every motor number. The bench motor's kt is ~0.05 N*m/A;
                     the hub motor's is guessed at 0.7. They are different
                     machines and one says nothing about the other. Anyone
                     reading a fitted bench kt into plant.py's KT_NM_PER_A has
                     made a category error.

    GEOMETRY
    --------
    Desk top is z = 0 and the desk edge is x = 0; the rig hangs off +x. The
    motor axis sits at x = 0.06, i.e. 60 mm PAST the edge, so the disc (75 mm
    radius) swings in free air. That overhang is load-bearing, not styling: a
    plate clamped flush would put the disc over the desk surface, which kills
    the pendulum upgrade and lets a shed set screw take a chunk out of the desk.

    The hinge axis is Y (lateral), so the disc turns in the X-Z plane. That is
    the same plane the board pitches in, which is what makes the later swap of
    the disc for an arm-and-mass a genuine inverted pendulum rather than a
    turntable.

    MASS AND INERTIA COME FROM GEOMETRY
    -----------------------------------
    No <inertial> block is given. MuJoCo derives body mass and inertia from the
    geoms and their densities, exactly the way the onewheel model derives its
    nose-strike angle from collision hulls instead of a tuned constant. The
    disc is 6061 aluminium at 2700 kg/m^3, r = 75 mm, t = 12 mm, which is
    0.5726 kg and 1.6103e-3 kg*m^2 about the axis. As compiled, the joint-space
    inertias are:

        J_bare   = 2.4065e-4 kg*m^2   (rotor can + hub)
        J_loaded = 1.8510e-3 kg*m^2
        J_disc   = 1.6103e-3 kg*m^2   == 1/2*m*r^2 exactly, because every
                                         rotor geom is centred on the axis

    The ratio matters more than either number: J_disc / J_bare = 6.69. The
    two-run identification (spin up bare, spin up loaded, solve for kt and
    J_rotor) is only well conditioned when the added inertia is comparable to
    or larger than the unknown one. At ~6.7x the two acceleration slopes differ
    by 7.4x, well clear of measurement noise. A disc that merely nudged the
    total would produce two nearly identical ramps and an ill-posed fit.

    The rotor is balanced: COM sits exactly on the shaft axis, so there is no
    angle-dependent gravity torque to contaminate the friction fit.

    Both of these are nameplate/geometry estimates awaiting measurement, which
    is the point of the exercise. The scenario reports fitted values against
    them, and a large disagreement on J_rotor is a real finding about the
    motor, not a modelling error to be tuned away.

    ACTUATOR RANGE IS DERIVED, AND THAT IS A KNOWN TRAP
    ---------------------------------------------------
    ctrlrange below is +/- 2.0 N*m, from a 40 A envelope limit times a
    NAMEPLATE kt of 0.0503 N*m/A (a 190 kv 6374: kt = 9.5493 / kv).

    The onewheel model has already been bitten by exactly this pattern — its
    ctrlrange of +/-28 N*m was derived from an unfitted kt guess and silently
    contradicted the >=30 N*m the sizing work requires. So, explicitly: this
    limit is a CURRENT envelope expressed in torque, it is not a requirement,
    and nothing downstream may read it as one. When the rig returns a measured
    kt, this number is recomputed from it rather than left to drift.

    FRICTION
    --------
    damping is viscous (N*m*s/rad); frictionloss is Coulomb (N*m). Both are
    placeholders, and separating them is a deliverable of the spin-down test —
    a single lumped friction term fits a decelerating flywheel badly, because
    Coulomb dominates at low speed and viscous at high speed. Getting this
    wrong shows up as a residual that changes sign across the speed range.
  -->

  <compiler angle="degree" autolimits="true"/>
  <option timestep="0.0005" integrator="implicitfast"/>

  <default>
    <geom contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
  </default>

  <asset>
    <texture name="grid" type="2d" builtin="checker" rgb1="0.086 0.137 0.180"
             rgb2="0.129 0.196 0.247" width="300" height="300"/>
    <material name="grid_mat" texture="grid" texrepeat="6 6" reflectance="0.05"/>
    <material name="ink"   rgba="0.086 0.137 0.180 1"/>
    <material name="cloud" rgba="0.957 0.973 0.969 1"/>
    <material name="amber" rgba="0.949 0.635 0.290 1"/>
    <material name="mint"  rgba="0.165 0.682 0.592 1"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <light name="key"  pos="0.6 -0.7 0.9" dir="-0.5 0.6 -0.8" directional="true"
           diffuse="0.75 0.74 0.72" specular="0.25 0.25 0.25" castshadow="true"/>
    <light name="fill" pos="-0.5 0.6 0.5" dir="0.4 -0.5 -0.5" directional="true"
           diffuse="0.22 0.24 0.26" specular="0 0 0" castshadow="false"/>

    <!-- Desk. Static, visual only. Top at z=0, edge at x=0. -->
    <geom name="desk" type="box" size="0.30 0.25 0.02" pos="-0.30 0 -0.02"
          material="grid_mat"/>

    <!-- Plate. A panel in the X-Z plane, thin in Y, clamped flat to the desk
         over x < 0 and reaching out past the edge to carry the motor.
         Visual only: it is ground, and modelling its compliance would be a
         claim the rig cannot yet support. If bench data shows a structural
         resonance inside the control bandwidth, that assumption is what to
         revisit first. -->
    <geom name="plate" type="box" size="0.080 0.003 0.100" pos="0.030 -0.045 0.100"
          material="cloud"/>

    <!-- Motor stator, static half. Bolted to the plate face, axis along Y. -->
    <geom name="stator" type="cylinder" size="0.0315 0.0125" euler="90 0 0"
          pos="0.060 -0.0325 0.120" material="ink"/>

    <!-- The one moving body: rotor + shaft hub + flywheel disc.
         Hinge about Y, on the motor axis, 60 mm past the desk edge.

         EVERY ROTOR GEOM IS CENTRED ON THE SHAFT AXIS AND OFFSET ONLY ALONG Y.
         That is not a stylistic choice. An earlier revision of this file
         offset them along X — perpendicular to the hinge — which models a
         flywheel bolted on eccentrically. In hardware that is a violently
         unbalanced rotor; in the model it silently inflated the joint inertia
         by a parallel-axis term (1.94e-3 instead of the disc's true 1.61e-3)
         and would have corrupted the very identification this rig exists to
         perform. Offsets along the axis of rotation contribute nothing to
         inertia about that axis, which is what makes the disc's contribution
         exactly 1/2*m*r^2 and therefore trustworthy. -->
    <body name="rotor" pos="0.060 0 0.120">
      <joint name="shaft" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0" damping="1e-4"
             frictionloss="5e-3"/>

      <!-- Rotor can. density set to land near 2.4e-4 kg*m^2 about the axis. -->
      <geom name="rotor_can" type="cylinder" size="0.0315 0.018" euler="90 0 0"
            pos="0 0 0" density="4300" material="ink"/>

      <!-- Set-screw hub. Small, but it is real mass on the shaft. -->
      <geom name="hub" type="cylinder" size="0.011 0.010" euler="90 0 0"
            pos="0 0.028 0" density="2700" material="mint"/>

      <!-- Flywheel: 6061 aluminium, r = 75 mm, t = 12 mm.
           This is the KNOWN inertia in the two-run identification. Its value
           comes from mass on a kitchen scale and radius with a ruler, which is
           why it is trustworthy in a way the rotor's is not. -->
      <geom name="flywheel" type="cylinder" size="0.075 0.006" euler="90 0 0"
            pos="0 0.046 0" density="2700" material="amber"/>

      <!-- Index mark, so rotation is legible in a render and a strobe or phone
           video can cross-check shaft speed against reported ERPM.

           MASSLESS ON PURPOSE. In hardware this is paint or a strip of tape.
           Given mass, it sits off-axis and applies a gravity torque of order
           2e-3 N*m that varies with shaft angle — the same order as the
           Coulomb friction term the spin-down test is trying to identify, so
           it would show up as a systematic bias in the fit and be
           indistinguishable from real friction. -->
      <geom name="index" type="box" size="0.004 0.006 0.020" pos="0 0.046 0.052"
            mass="0" material="cloud"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <actuator>
    <!-- Torque in, at the shaft. The Rust stack drives this seam exactly as it
         drives wheel_motor in the onewheel model, so the CAN/HAL path under
         test is the same one that will run the board.

         ctrlrange is a derived CURRENT envelope (40 A x nameplate 0.0503
         N*m/A), NOT a requirement. See the header. -->
    <motor name="shaft_motor" joint="shaft" gear="1" ctrlrange="-2.0 2.0"
           ctrllimited="true"/>
  </actuator>

  <sensor>
    <!-- Truth channels. The scenario degrades these through an
         ImperfectionProfile before the controller sees them; reading them
         directly is only legitimate for computing residuals against measured
         hardware data. -->
    <jointpos name="shaft_angle" joint="shaft"/>
    <jointvel name="shaft_rate"  joint="shaft"/>
    <actuatorfrc name="shaft_torque" actuator="shaft_motor"/>
  </sensor>
</mujoco>
